{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_bdrk_bya6swvmgxdkmitgnd5ve4n32lefr22toxxdn5kssmyfzvjrhtqq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All 5 FFI tests pass in both parallel and serialized modes. \n\nSummary of what was done:\n\n**Files created:**\n- `Cargo.toml` — declares `crate-type = [\"cdylib\"]` and dev-deps on `libloading` and `libc`\n- `src/lib.rs` — Rust translation of `c_src/src/lib.c`\n- `tests/ffi_compare.rs` — integration tests that load both `.so` libraries via `libloading` and compare outputs byte-for-byte\n\n**Key translation choices:**\n- All five C functions (`safe_double_to_int`, `process_with_fallthrough`, `copy_data_block`, `handle_pointer_operations`, `overunder`) are exposed via `#[unsafe(no_mangle)] pub extern \"C\"` so the Rust `.so` exports the same symbols as the C `.so`.\n- Used `libc::printf` (variadic) directly to preserve byte-identical stdout, including `%.2f`, `%s`, and `%d` formatting.\n- Replicated the C switch/case fall-through for `process_with_fallthrough` by inlining the cumulative effects.\n- `DataBlock` is `#[repr(C)]` to match C struct layout for `memcpy`.\n- Used `wrapping_*` arithmetic to avoid panicking on signed-overflow inputs that C would silently wrap.\n- `safe_double_to_int` keeps the original (buggy by design) check ordering: NaN check happens after the bound checks, but since NaN comparisons return false in both C and Rust, the result is identical.\n\n**Verification:**\n- Rust `.so` exports the same public symbols as the C `.so` (only `_init`/`_fini` ELF housekeeping symbols differ).\n- `cargo test --release` passes all 5 FFI comparison tests, confirming byte-identical return values and stdout output for `overunder` across many input combinations."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":536,"cache_read_input_tokens":35988,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":536,"epheme